    debrief: 'The weak answer filters on mrr = 0 or plan name = \'Starter\' — which breaks the moment the plan is renamed or repriced. Filtering on p.tier = \'free\' is the stable business-logic approach. The second common mistake: forgetting s.status = \'active\', which would include accounts that have already churned off the free plan. Two accounts qualify: India Foods (9) and Lima Education (12).\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** omitting s.status = \'active\' → returns 5 rows instead of 2, including churned accounts 13, 14, and 15 which previously had free plans. The CSM team cold-calls churned companies — a credibility disaster. No error. Plausible-looking result. **Catch it:** add SELECT DISTINCT s.status FROM your result join to verify all returned subscriptions are active.\n\n**Sanity check:** your result should have exactly 2 rows. Cross-check: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM subscriptions s JOIN plans p ON s.plan_id=p.plan_id WHERE p.tier=\'free\' AND s.status=\'active\' = 2. If you get a different number, you have a join or filter problem.',
