    checkValues: [{ user_id: '1', username: 'alice', launch_count: '3' }],
    solution: 'SELECT u.user_id, u.username, COUNT(*) AS launch_count\nFROM events e\nJOIN users u ON e.user_id = u.user_id\nWHERE e.event_name = \'app_open\'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.username\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2\nORDER BY launch_count DESC',
    debrief: 'Four users qualify: alice (3 opens), bob (2), carol (2), emma (2) — only 4 of 15 registered users (27%) have opened the app more than once. That\'s a retention signal worth surfacing to the product team before any other analysis. The core SQL concept: HAVING filters on aggregated results after GROUP BY; WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation. Writing WHERE COUNT(*) >= 2 is a syntax error — COUNT doesn\'t exist yet at the WHERE stage. A common workaround wraps everything in a subquery and filters the outer query: correct, but verbose. HAVING is the clean single-pass solution. Senior follow-up: "What if we only want users who opened on at least 2 distinct calendar days?" — change HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 to HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT DATE(occurred_at)) >= 2. Same structure, different business question, one word changed.\n\n**Before writing:** confirm what "more than once" means. "2+ app_open events total" (current) counts a user who opened twice in one minute. "2+ distinct calendar days" is a stronger retention signal. These produce the same 4 users here but diverge on real data with power users who open the app dozens of times per day.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** removing the WHERE event_name=\'app_open\' filter → counts all event types, not just launches. Users who liked or shared content but never re-opened the app appear as "retained." Alice has 5 total events but only 3 app_opens. The launch count is wrong and the retention metric is meaningless. **Catch it:** spot-check one user: alice should have exactly 3 app_opens. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM events WHERE user_id=1 AND event_name=\'app_open\' = 3.\n\n**Sanity check:** result should have 4 rows. SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FROM events WHERE event_name=\'app_open\' GROUP BY user_id HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 → 4 user_ids. If your result differs, check the event_name filter.',
