    debrief: 'Dr. Smith has a 40% no-show rate (4 out of 10 appointments) — the highest in the practice. GROUP BY p.provider_id, p.name is the correct form: grouping on name alone would incorrectly merge two providers who share a name. AVG(no_show) is numerically equivalent here since no_show is 0/1, but the SUM/COUNT idiom is the interviewer-preferred form because it is explicit and generalizes. The follow-up: "Is 40% actually high? What context do you need?" — benchmarks by specialty, appointment type mix, patient risk factors.\n\n**Before writing:** "no-show rate" has two valid denominators: (a) all scheduled appointments — current implementation; (b) only appointments that have already occurred (excluding future or cancelled). On real data with future appointments in the table, including them inflates the denominator and makes the rate look artificially low. Confirm which the operations team wants before writing.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** ROUND(SUM(a.no_show) / COUNT(*), 1) — missing 100.0. Integer division: 4/10 = 0 in SQL. Every provider returns no_show_rate = 0.0. The output has the right columns, the right row count, the right structure — and every single rate is wrong. This is the most common silent rate calculation bug. **Catch it:** Dr. Smith\'s rate should be 40.0. If it\'s 0.0, you\'re missing the 100.0 multiplier or the CAST.\n\n**Sanity check:** Dr. Smith (provider_id=1) has 4 no-shows across appts 3, 5, 7, 9. Verify: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM appointments WHERE provider_id=1 AND no_show=1 = 4. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM appointments WHERE provider_id=1 = 10. Rate = 40.0%.',
