    debrief: 'Shopping dominates at 14 transactions (35%), followed by food (9), travel (7), entertainment (6), utilities (4). Shopping\'s high share partly reflects that flagged merchants are categorized as \'shopping\' — a categorization artifact. A cleaner analysis would separate flagged-merchant transactions from legitimate retail purchases before ranking categories by risk-adjusted volume.\n\n**Before writing:** "transaction volume" is ambiguous — it could mean count of transactions (current) or total amount transacted. These produce completely different rankings: entertainment ranks 4th by count but has the lowest average amount ($12.66); travel ranks 3rd by count but has some of the largest transactions ($680+). Confirm which metric the analytics team actually needs before writing.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** SUM(amount) instead of COUNT(*) → shopping still leads (highest total spend) but the ranking order for other categories changes. The analytics team asks "which categories have the most activity" — you give them "which categories have the most spend." Both are valid metrics with the same name "volume." **Catch it:** SUM of all txn_count values = SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions = 40. 14+9+7+6+4=40. If your sum doesn\'t equal 40, your GROUP BY has a gap.\n\n**Sanity check:** exactly 5 rows — one per distinct category. SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT category) FROM transactions = 5.',
