    checkValues: [{ country: 'AU', is_premium: '0', user_count: '1' }],
    solution: 'SELECT country, is_premium, COUNT(*) AS user_count\nFROM users\nGROUP BY country, is_premium\nORDER BY country, is_premium',
    debrief: 'The US has 4 premium users and 6 free users — a 40% conversion rate. Every other country has a single user, all either premium (GB, CA) or free (AU, DE, IN). Multi-column GROUP BY is the key concept: GROUP BY country, is_premium gives one row per unique (country, tier) pair, not one row per country. GROUP BY country alone would collapse the tier dimension and lose the premium/free split entirely. A common mistake is grouping only by country and then trying to filter by is_premium — that discards the segmentation you need. The pattern of two-dimensional GROUP BY (segment × attribute) is used constantly in growth and product analytics: DAU by platform by cohort, revenue by region by plan, engagement by device by feature.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** GROUP BY country only → collapses the is_premium dimension and returns 1 row per country with total user_count. US shows 10, not the 4/6 premium/free split. The growth team thinks they\'re seeing tier adoption by country; they\'re seeing headcount by country. No error. Wrong granularity. **Catch it:** your result should have more rows than distinct countries. SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT country) FROM users = 5; your result should have 7 rows. If result rows = distinct countries, GROUP BY is missing is_premium.\n\n**Sanity check:** SUM of all user_count values in your result = SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users. Here: 4+6+1+1+1+1+1 = 15. If the sum doesn\'t match, your GROUP BY has a gap or a fanout.',
