    solution: 'SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS buyer_count\nFROM orders',
    debrief: '12 of 15 registered users have placed at least one order — an 80% buyer activation rate. Users 13, 14, and 15 have never ordered. COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts each user once regardless of how many orders they placed. COUNT(user_id) would return 28 (total orders), and COUNT(*) also returns 28. DISTINCT is the critical keyword here for a unique-user count.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders → returns 28 (total orders placed, not unique buyers). The CMO reports "28 buyers" to the board. The actual number is 12. Buyer activation rate is presented as 28/15 = 187% — logically impossible and immediately credibility-destroying. No error. Single-number output. Completely wrong. **Catch it:** if your result is > 15 (total users in the table), you\'re counting orders not buyers. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users = 15. buyer_count can never exceed this.\n\n**Sanity check:** buyer_count + never-ordered users = total users. 12 + 3 = 15. Verify: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT DISTINCT user_id FROM orders) = 3.',
    sqliteNote: null,
