    debrief: 'US users (1,3,5,7,9) placed 15 of the 28 total orders (54%). The JOIN on user_id connects orders to user attributes — without it, you can\'t filter by country since orders only stores user_id. This is the fundamental reason to JOIN rather than filter the orders table directly: customer attributes live in users, not orders.\n\n**Forensic trap:** A common mistake is querying only the orders table with no JOIN, then trying to filter on country — but orders has no country column, so the analyst adds it as WHERE user_id IN (1,3,5,7,9) by hardcoding IDs from memory. That returns 15 rows with the right count but hardcoded values break the moment users change country or new US users are added. Alternatively, an analyst who forgets to filter entirely returns all 28 orders — a number that looks plausible as a "total orders" figure but is the wrong scope.\n\n**Sanity check:** Count distinct user_ids in your result — it should be exactly 5 (users 1, 3, 5, 7, 9). If you see more than 5, you\'ve either pulled non-US users or the country filter is missing. Cross-check: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders gives 28 total; your query should return 15, which is 53.6% of total.',
