    solution: 'SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.category, t.occurred_at, m.name, m.country\nFROM transactions t\nJOIN merchants m ON t.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE m.country != \'US\'\nORDER BY t.occurred_at',
    debrief: 'Eight transactions occurred at non-US merchants: Shell Gas (UK), QuickTransfer (Nigeria ×2), CryptoXchange (China), FastCash Ltd (Brazil ×2), Spotify (Sweden ×2). The three flagged merchants (NG, CN, BR) account for 5 of the 8 cross-border transactions. Cross-border by itself is not fraud — Spotify (SE) is entirely legitimate — so flagging must combine geography with merchant risk score. Critical compliance gap: merchant_id is NULL for P2P (peer-to-peer) transfers. The INNER JOIN silently excludes all P2P transactions — they never appear in results regardless of amount or country. In production, cross-border compliance must separately audit P2P transactions: WHERE merchant_id IS NULL. A LEFT JOIN with m.country != \'US\' would partially capture them but requires careful handling since m.country is NULL for P2P. The correct production query uses two separate CTEs: one for merchant transactions, one for P2P transfers, then UNION ALL.\n\n**Forensic trap:** A common mistake is omitting the JOIN entirely and filtering WHERE category = \'travel\' or confusing the transactions.category column (food/travel/shopping) with merchant country. An analyst scanning the transactions table might write WHERE category = \'travel\' thinking that captures cross-border activity — that returns travel-category transactions including US merchants like Delta Airlines, which is entirely wrong.\n\n**Sanity check:** Check that all 8 rows have a non-US country value in the country column. Verify 5 distinct merchants: Shell Gas (UK ×1), QuickTransfer (NG ×2), CryptoXchange (CN ×1), FastCash Ltd (BR ×2), Spotify (SE ×2). Cross-check: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions gives 40 total; US-merchant transactions should be 32, so non-US should be 8.',
