    debrief: 'Likes (2) outnumber shares (1) — consistent with shares being a higher-friction, higher-intent action. Only 3 high-intent events across 30 total events (10%) — a signal worth tracking over time. WHERE event_name IN (\'like\', \'share\') is the clean two-value filter; the alternative WHERE event_name = \'like\' OR event_name = \'share\' is identical in result but harder to extend. If the attribution model added comments as high-intent, IN just needs a third value. IN with literals also makes a natural self-documenting list: when you read it, you immediately know the full set of included values. An IN subquery (WHERE event_name IN (SELECT ...)) is a different and more advanced construct — this problem uses a literal list only.\n\n**Forensic trap:** A common mistake is omitting the WHERE filter entirely and counting all event types — running SELECT event_name, COUNT(*) AS event_count FROM events GROUP BY event_name ORDER BY event_count DESC. That returns 6 rows (app_open, content_view, purchase, comment, like, share) with app_open at 15 and content_view at 9. The like and share rows still appear in the output but so do 4 other irrelevant event types, and the problem asks for only the 2 high-intent types. The result looks informative but answers a different question.\n\n**Sanity check:** Your result must have exactly 2 rows. Verify by summing event_count values — like (2) + share (1) = 3 total. Cross-check: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM events WHERE event_name IN (\'like\', \'share\') should return 3. If your sum is anything other than 3, the WHERE filter is wrong or missing.',
