    checkValues: [{ tier: 'enterprise', total_mrr: '8997', sub_count: '3' }],
    solution: 'SELECT p.tier, SUM(s.mrr) AS total_mrr, COUNT(*) AS sub_count\nFROM subscriptions s\nJOIN plans p ON s.plan_id = p.plan_id\nWHERE s.status = \'active\'\nGROUP BY p.tier\nORDER BY total_mrr DESC',
    debrief: 'Enterprise tier: 3 subscriptions, $8,997 MRR (76% of total). Paid tier: 7 subscriptions, $2,793. Free tier: 2 subscriptions, $0. The two free-tier subscriptions confirm the upsell target list from earlier — India Foods and Lima Education. The key technique here: two aggregate functions in the same SELECT (SUM and COUNT) over the same GROUP BY. They are computed independently and simultaneously — no subquery needed. SUM aggregates the mrr value per tier; COUNT aggregates the row count per tier. Returning both in one query is more efficient than running two separate GROUP BY queries. The WHERE pre-filters to active subscriptions; without it, churned subscriptions (status = \'churned\') would be included, overstating historical MRR as current MRR.\n\n**Forensic trap:** A common mistake is omitting the WHERE status = \'active\' filter. Without it, the paid tier shows 12 subscriptions and $6,388 total_mrr instead of 7 and $2,793 — because 5 churned paid-tier subscriptions (sub_ids 1, 3, 14, 15, 16) are counted in. The enterprise tier happens to be unaffected (all 3 enterprise subs are active), so the enterprise row looks correct while the paid row is badly inflated. An analyst checking only the enterprise row would miss the error entirely.\n\n**Sanity check:** Sum all total_mrr values across your 3 tier rows — they should equal $11,790. Then verify sub_count values sum to 12 (3 enterprise + 7 paid + 2 free). Cross-check: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM subscriptions WHERE status = \'active\' should return 12. If sub_count sums to 17 (all subscriptions including churned), the status filter is missing.',
