    solution: 'SELECT order_id, user_id, subtotal, discount,\n  ROUND((subtotal - discount)::numeric, 2) AS effective_price\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = \'completed\'\n  AND discount > 0\nORDER BY effective_price DESC',
    debrief: 'Nine completed orders had a discount applied. Order 9 (eve, Smart Watch) leads with the largest absolute discount ($50) and the highest effective price ($219.99). The key SQL concept: arithmetic in SELECT computes a new derived column that does not exist in the table. ROUND(subtotal - discount, 2) prevents floating-point display issues. Two conditions in WHERE: status = \'completed\' excludes cancelled orders where the discount was never realized, and discount > 0 excludes full-price orders. The weak answer omits the completed filter — a cancelled $50-discount order was never paid, so including it overstates promotion redemption. Always check whether your dataset filters to realized events, not just recorded ones.\n\n**Forensic trap:** Omitting the status = \'completed\' filter returns 11 rows instead of 9 — it includes cancelled order 4 (bob, $249.99, no discount applied so discount > 0 still excludes it) and returned order 7 (dan, $34.99, no discount) — actually those have discount = 0 so they are excluded by the second condition. The real leakage is order 16 (grace, cancelled, discount = 0) is excluded anyway. The dangerous case: order 4 has discount = 0 so it stays out. But if a future cancelled order has a non-zero discount, it would appear — the filter gap is a latent correctness risk, not just a current one.\n\n**Sanity check:** Verify the row count is 9. Confirm order 9 (subtotal 249.99, discount 30.00, effective_price 219.99) is the first row. Check that order 4 (cancelled, no discount) is absent — it should be excluded by both the status and discount filters.',
