    debrief: 'Completed orders dominate (23 of 28). Cancelled orders average higher than returns — both cancelled orders are high-value ($249.99 and $89.99), suggesting customers cancel expensive purchases rather than go through the return process. Three aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG) run simultaneously over the same GROUP BY: COUNT tallies rows, SUM accumulates the numeric column, AVG divides that sum by the count. All three are computed in a single scan — no subqueries, no CTEs. The pattern of "report all three" is a standard finance analyst deliverable: order_count for volume, total_subtotal for gross revenue exposure, avg_subtotal for order health. ROUND on SUM prevents floating-point display noise on the total.\n\n**Forensic trap:** Omitting ROUND on avg_subtotal returns values like 106.5217391304348 instead of 106.52 — not a logical error but a presentation failure that gets a CV rejected in a take-home. More critically, using COUNT(subtotal) instead of COUNT(*) returns the same result here (subtotal is never NULL) but is semantically wrong: COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(column) counts non-NULL values — a distinction that breaks silently the moment a NULL appears.\n\n**Sanity check:** Verify 3 rows (completed, cancelled, returned). Confirm completed order_count = 23 and avg_subtotal = 106.52. Cross-check: SUM of all completed subtotals divided by 23 should equal 106.52.',
