    solution: 'SELECT p.category,\n  ROUND((SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price))::numeric, 2) AS total_revenue\nFROM order_items oi\nJOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.product_id\nGROUP BY p.category\nORDER BY total_revenue DESC',
    debrief: 'The bug is missing quantity in the revenue formula. SUM(oi.unit_price) treats every order item as 1 unit — a Smart Watch in a 4-unit order contributes $249.99 instead of $999.96. The correct formula is SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price). Electronics drops from $569.95 to the correct $1,719.87. The ranking held by coincidence, which is why nobody caught it — the story the data told was plausible.\n\n**Why it passed review:** The category order (electronics first) matched expectations. Only the absolute values were wrong, and most people checking the report were looking at relative performance, not verifying absolute totals.\n\n**The sanity check that would have caught it:** Total revenue across all categories should equal SELECT SUM(quantity * unit_price) FROM order_items. The broken total is ~$1,700. The correct total is ~$2,910. A 40% discrepancy would have been visible if anyone had cross-checked.',
