    priority: 1,
    estimatedMin: 8,
    datamartId: 'saas',
    prompt: 'The win-back team sends a re-engagement campaign to every account in the churned subscription list. Last week they emailed Acme Corp — currently on your highest-tier Enterprise plan at $2,999/month — a "we miss you" discount offer. The account manager escalated immediately. Find the bug and write a corrected query that returns only accounts with no current active subscription.',
    brokenQuery: 'SELECT s.sub_id, s.account_id, s.ended_at, s.mrr\nFROM subscriptions s\nWHERE s.status = \'churned\'\nORDER BY s.ended_at',
    brokenOutputNote: 'Returns 5 rows including Acme Corp (account 1), which churned its original Business plan but immediately re-subscribed at Enterprise. Also includes account 3 (downgraded to Growth, still active). The win-back list has 2 false positives.',
    expectedColumns: ['sub_id', 'account_id', 'ended_at', 'mrr'],
    expectedRowCount: 3,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider how to identify accounts that have no active subscriptions. Think about filtering based on subscription status and account relationships.', starterCode: 'SELECT account_id FROM subscriptions WHERE status = \'churned\' -- add filter condition' },
      { text: 'Use a subquery or a NOT EXISTS clause to exclude accounts with active subscriptions. Ensure you\'re comparing the correct account relationships.', starterCode: 'SELECT s.account_id FROM subscriptions s WHERE s.status = \'churned\' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM subscriptions s2 WHERE s2.account_id = s.account_id AND s2.status = \'active\')' }
    ],
    hints: ['The query correctly finds churned subscriptions. What additional condition would exclude accounts that have a current active subscription?', 'Look at NOT EXISTS or a LEFT JOIN anti-join pattern.'],
    checkValues: [{ sub_id: '14', account_id: '13' }],
    solution: 'SELECT s.sub_id, s.account_id, s.ended_at, s.mrr\nFROM subscriptions s\nWHERE s.status = \'churned\'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM subscriptions s2\n    WHERE s2.account_id = s.account_id\n      AND s2.status = \'active\'\n  )\nORDER BY s.ended_at',
    debrief: 'The bug is not in the SQL syntax — it\'s in the analytical frame. "Churned subscription" ≠ "lost customer." Account 1 has a churned Business sub AND an active Enterprise sub (the upgrade path). Account 3 has a churned Business sub AND an active Growth sub (a downgrade, not true churn). The win-back list must exclude any account with a current active subscription.\n\n**This is the most important forensic class:** the query is syntactically correct and the output is a valid dataset. The error is in what the analyst believes the query measures versus what it actually measures.\n\n**The fix:** NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM subscriptions s2 WHERE s2.account_id = s.account_id AND s2.status = \'active\'). The three truly churned accounts are 13, 14, and 15 — no active subscription anywhere. Sub 14 (account 13, Business at $999) is the first in the result.\n\n**Production rule:** Before any customer-facing campaign list ships, verify every account on it does not have an active relationship. A win-back email to an active customer is a credibility incident.',
