    solution: 'SELECT ROUND((SUM(subtotal))::numeric, 2) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = \'completed\'',
    debrief: 'The bug is a JOIN fanout. Orders 14 and 23 each have two line items in order_items. When you JOIN orders to order_items, each of those orders produces two rows — and SUM(o.subtotal) counts the order subtotal once per matching item row. Order 14 ($39.99) is counted twice, order 23 ($89.99) is counted twice: total inflation = $129.96.\n\n**Why it passes review:** The query looks correct — joining to order_items seems natural when doing revenue analysis. The inflated result ($2,459.75) is plausible. Nobody questioned a 6% discrepancy until a payment processor cross-check caught it.\n\n**The fix:** orders.subtotal already contains the correct per-order total. Joining to order_items is unnecessary for order-level revenue. If you need line-item detail, aggregate in a subquery first (SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) per order), then join to orders.\n\n**The rule:** any time you JOIN a table that has a one-to-many relationship with your fact table, verify whether your SUM is inflating. The check: count rows before and after the JOIN — if COUNT(*) increases, so will every non-aggregated SUM.',
    sqliteNote: null,
