    datamartId: 'marketplace',
    prompt: 'The seller performance report is missing one active seller. The operations team has 10 active sellers, but the report shows only 9. This seller was onboarded but has never had a completed transaction, and their listing was disputed and refunded. Identify why this seller is missing from the report and correct the query.',
    brokenQuery: 'SELECT s.name,\n  ROUND((SUM(t.sale_price))::numeric, 2) AS total_gmv\nFROM sellers s\nJOIN transactions t ON s.seller_id = t.seller_id\nWHERE t.status = \'completed\'\nGROUP BY s.seller_id, s.name\nORDER BY total_gmv DESC',
    brokenOutputNote: 'Returns 9 rows. GadgetWorld (seller_id=6) has only a refunded transaction — the INNER JOIN combined with WHERE status=\'completed\' silently drops them from the report. Finance has never seen GadgetWorld in any revenue report.',
    expectedColumns: ['name', 'total_gmv'],
    expectedRowCount: 10,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider the relationship between sellers and their completed transactions. Think about how to ensure all sellers are included, even if they have no completed transactions.', starterCode: 'SELECT s.seller_id, s.name\nFROM sellers s\n-- add filter condition\n-- complete the JOIN' },
      { text: 'Examine how to aggregate transaction data for each seller while ensuring all sellers are accounted for, including those with no completed transactions.', starterCode: 'SELECT s.seller_id, s.name,\n  COALESCE(ROUND(SUM(t.sale_price), 2), 0) AS total_gmv\nFROM sellers s\n-- your window function here\nGROUP BY s.seller_id, s.name' }
    ],
    hints: ['Which JOIN type keeps all rows from the left table regardless of whether a match exists?', 'When you filter on a right-table column in WHERE after a LEFT JOIN, what happens to rows where the right table had no match?'],
    checkValues: [{ name: 'TechVault', total_gmv: '1487' }],
    solution: 'SELECT s.name,\n  COALESCE(ROUND((SUM(t.sale_price))::numeric, 2), 0) AS total_gmv\nFROM sellers s\nLEFT JOIN transactions t ON s.seller_id = t.seller_id\n  AND t.status = \'completed\'\nGROUP BY s.seller_id, s.name\nORDER BY total_gmv DESC',
    debrief: 'Two bugs work together. First: INNER JOIN (written as plain JOIN) drops any seller with no matching transaction. Second: the WHERE t.status=\'completed\' filter is placed after the JOIN — even if you change to LEFT JOIN, the WHERE clause converts it back to INNER JOIN behavior by requiring a non-NULL status column on the right side.\n\n**The fix has two parts:** change JOIN to LEFT JOIN, and move the status filter from WHERE into the JOIN\'s ON condition (AND t.status=\'completed\'). This keeps all sellers in the result while only summing completed transactions for those who have them.\n\n**Why this matters operationally:** GadgetWorld has been onboarded, has active listings, and has attempted a sale (that was refunded). They are a real seller. A seller performance report that omits them means the ops team can\'t monitor their activity, identify issues early, or flag the refund pattern.\n\n**The rule:** any time you want "all rows from table A with optional data from table B", use LEFT JOIN and put B\'s filter conditions in ON, not WHERE. WHERE always runs after JOIN and will eliminate NULL rows from the left table.',
