    checkValues: [{ month: '2023-03', order_count: '1' }],
    solution: 'SELECT to_char((created_at)::timestamp, \'YYYY-MM\') AS month,\n  COUNT(*) AS order_count\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY to_char((created_at)::timestamp, \'YYYY-MM\')\nORDER BY month',
    debrief: 'The bug is using strftime(\'%m\') instead of strftime(\'%Y-%m\'). The %m format extracts the month number (01–12) with no year. Any month that appears in both 2023 and 2024 is collapsed into a single row. March is the only such month in this dataset — 1 order in March 2023 and 11 orders in March 2024 get merged into a single "03" row with 12 orders.\n\n**Why the chart looked plausible:** March is often a strong month in food delivery (spring weather, promotions). A spike in March reads as seasonal behavior, not a data artifact. The PM\'s volume sanity check ("more than Jan+Feb combined") was the right instinct.\n\n**The corrected query** produces 9 rows: months from 2023-03 through 2024-03 with a gap in 2023-10 through 2023-12 (no orders in that period). March 2024 correctly shows 11 orders as a standalone month.\n\n**The rule:** when grouping time-series data, always include the year. strftime(\'%Y-%m\') is the correct pattern for monthly grouping. strftime(\'%m\') should only appear when you explicitly want cross-year aggregation (e.g., seasonality analysis).',
