    hints: ['What is the difference between UNION ALL and UNION?', 'How do you count each user exactly once even if they appear in both channels?'],
    checkValues: [{ total_reached_users: '10' }],
    solution: 'SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS total_reached_users\nFROM sessions\nWHERE source IN (\'paid\', \'organic\')',
    debrief: 'Two bugs compound. First: UNION ALL preserves duplicates — a user with sessions in both channels appears in both subqueries. Second: COUNT(*) counts rows, not distinct users. User 2 has 2 paid sessions, user 5 has 2 paid + 1 organic session, user 11 has 2 paid sessions. The total row count (15) happens to equal the user count by coincidence — masking the error.\n\n**The correct count:** 10 distinct users have at least one paid or organic session (users 1,2,3,5,6,9,10,11,13,15). The fix is a single query with COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) — no subquery needed.\n\n**The 100% reach red flag:** any campaign reach number ≥ total user count is a strong signal of duplicate counting. The PM\'s skepticism ("that\'s impossible") was the correct instinct.\n\n**UNION vs UNION ALL:** UNION deduplicates rows before combining. UNION ALL keeps every row. In analytics, UNION is correct when you want distinct users across segments. UNION ALL is correct when you want to stack fact tables that genuinely have separate rows (e.g., two different event types that cannot overlap). For user-level distinct counts, COUNT(DISTINCT) over a single filtered table is always cleaner than UNION.',
