    solution: 'SELECT a.account_id, a.user_id, a.account_type, SUM(d.amount) AS total_disputed\nFROM disputes d\nJOIN transactions t ON d.txn_id = t.txn_id\nJOIN accounts a ON t.account_id = a.account_id\nGROUP BY a.account_id, a.user_id, a.account_type\nORDER BY a.account_id',
    debrief: 'The broken query joins disputes directly to accounts using ON d.dispute_id = a.account_id — matching the dispute\'s row number to the account number. Dispute 1 connects to account 1, dispute 2 to account 2, and so on. This is numerically coincidental: dispute IDs and account IDs are both small integers, so the join runs without error and returns 6 rows that look plausible.\n\n**The correct path:** Disputes link to transactions (via txn_id), and transactions link to accounts (via account_id). The join chain must traverse both relationships: disputes to transactions to accounts.\n\n**The evidence:** Dispute 1 is for $280 (transaction 7, a shopping purchase). Transaction 7 belongs to account 4 (user 3). The broken query assigns it to account 1 (user 1) — a completely different customer. In a compliance context, this misdirects investigation to the wrong account holder.\n\n**Sanity check:** Verify one row manually: find dispute 1 in the disputes table, look up txn_id 7 in transactions, check the account_id. If your result shows dispute 1 under account 1, the join is wrong.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** "If the same account had both a resolved and an open dispute, how would you separate them in this report?"',
