    debrief: 'The INNER JOIN silently excludes product 10 (Plant Pot) because it has no matching rows in order_items. The query asks for all products "including those with zero orders" — which requires a LEFT JOIN that keeps all products regardless of whether they appear in order_items.\n\n**The trap:** The query runs without error and returns 9 rows with correct counts for all ordered products. The missing row is invisible unless you cross-reference the result against the products table. If the analyst doesn\'t know the total catalog size is 10, the missing product is never noticed.\n\n**COUNT(*) vs COUNT(column):** With a LEFT JOIN, unmatched products get NULL for oi.item_id. COUNT(*) would count that NULL row and report 1 instead of 0. COUNT(oi.item_id) correctly ignores NULLs and returns 0. Both the JOIN type and the COUNT argument matter.\n\n**Business consequence:** A merchandising report that excludes zero-order products systematically hides dead inventory. Product 10 is the exact item that needs attention — it\'s been in the catalog but never ordered. An INNER JOIN makes it invisible to the very report designed to surface it.\n\n**Sanity check:** Check that your result includes exactly 10 rows — one per product. Product 10 (Plant Pot) should appear with times_ordered = 0.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** "If you wanted to see only the products that have never been ordered, how would you modify this query?"',
